## 4. Possible Errors & Troubleshooting

| Issue / Error | Cause | Solution |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'stage')`** | Attempting to access `ctx.tx.stage(...)` without chaining `.transactional(db)`. | Make sure you call `.transactional(db)` right after `Pipeline.send(context)`. |
| **Data not written to D1** | Action called `ctx.tx.db.insert(...)` or `.prepare(...)` directly instead of staging via `ctx.tx.stage(...)`. | In transactional pipelines, all writes must be passed to `ctx.tx.stage(stmt)`. Immediate writes bypass atomic batching and won't be rolled back on error. |
| **Unhandled Promise / Floating Promise** | Forgetting to `await` the pipeline call (`const res = Pipeline.send(...)`). | Always use `await Pipeline.send(...)...` or return the promise. |
| **State mutations lost between actions** | An action forgot to return `context` or returned `undefined`. | Ensure every pipe function returns `context` or `{ ...context, newProp: value }`. |
| **Cloudflare D1 batch size exceeded (`D1_ERROR`)** | Staging more than 100 statements or exceeding D1 payload limits in a single batch. | Split massive migrations into chunks of 50-100 items before executing the pipeline. |
| **Preventive Rollback behavior** | If an action throws an error, the execution immediately bubbles up the exception without calling `db.batch()`. | This is intended behavior. Handle the exception in your route middleware or `try/catch` wrapper. |
